T32.01|GDP measures production within an economy over a period under national-accounting conventions. Nominal growth includes price effects; real growth adjusts for them. Productivity describes output relative to inputs and can support lasting increases in living standards. Business cycles describe fluctuations around longer-term activity.~~GDP is not a company's revenue or a complete welfare measure. Composition, distribution and sustainability matter alongside the headline growth rate.|Nominal GDP: Output valued at current prices.;;Real GDP: Output adjusted for price changes.;;Productivity/cycle: Efficiency and fluctuations in activity.|Nominal output rises from 100 to 110 while the relevant price index rises 5%.~~Real growth is approximately 110 ÷ 1.05 ÷ 100 − 1 = 4.76%.;;Separate price and volume effects.;;Inspect which sectors contributed.~~Nominal growth can exceed growth in real production.~~Official measures use detailed methodologies beyond this simple example.|Check nominal versus real data.;;Inspect composition and revisions.;;Connect productivity to sustainable growth.
T32.02|Inflation is a rise in an aggregate price index; disinflation is slower positive inflation; deflation is falling aggregate prices. Individual goods can behave differently from the overall index. Expected inflation influences wages, pricing and nominal interest rates.~~Lower inflation does not usually mean prices return to earlier levels. Base effects can change annual rates even when monthly prices keep rising.|Inflation: Aggregate prices rise.;;Disinflation: Rate of increase slows.;;Deflation: Aggregate price level declines.|An index rises from 100 to 110, then to 115.5.~~First-year inflation is 10%.;;Second-year inflation is 5%.;;Prices are still 15.5% above the starting level.~~Disinflation slows increases rather than reversing them.~~A household's spending mix can experience different inflation.|Distinguish level from rate.;;Check index and horizon.;;Analyse wages, margins and purchasing power.
T32.03|Employment, wages and consumption connect household income with demand. More jobs can increase spending, while real wage growth depends on pay relative to prices. Unemployment measures exclude or classify some people differently from a broad count of those without work.~~Participation, hours and employment quality can change the interpretation of headline labour data. Consumers may save additional income or use it to repay debt.|Employment: People working under the statistical definition.;;Real wages: Pay adjusted for purchasing power.;;Consumption: Household spending on goods and services.|Nominal wages rise 6% while prices rise 8%.~~Real wage change is 1.06 ÷ 1.08 − 1 ≈ −1.85%.;;Assess whether more hours or jobs offset that loss.;;Evaluate consumption, saving and debt responses.~~Higher nominal pay can accompany lower purchasing power.~~Aggregate averages can conceal unequal outcomes.|Read labour definitions.;;Use real as well as nominal wages.;;Inspect participation, hours and household balance sheets.
T32.04|Money supply describes selected monetary aggregates; credit records borrowing claims; liquidity concerns ability to transact or fund obligations. Central-bank reserves, bank deposits and broad credit are related but not interchangeable. Lending, repayment and financial conditions influence economic spending.~~More liquidity does not mechanically create equal consumer-price inflation: demand, supply, expectations and the financial system mediate the effect.|Monetary base: Currency and relevant central-bank balances.;;Broad money: Defined deposit and currency aggregates.;;Credit: Borrowing claims across relevant sectors.|A bank makes an illustrative ₹10 lakh loan and credits the borrower's deposit.~~Loan assets rise ₹10.;;Deposit liabilities rise ₹10.;;Later repayment reduces both under the simplified transaction.~~Bank lending can create deposits within the accounting mechanism.~~Capital, liquidity, demand and regulation constrain actual lending.|Use precise aggregate definitions.;;Separate reserves from deposits.;;Analyse credit demand and balance-sheet constraints.
T32.05|Monetary policy influences economic conditions through rates, credit, asset prices, expectations and exchange rates. These channels operate with uncertain lags and vary across borrowers and financial systems. Policy aims do not guarantee immediate control of every price or market rate.~~Fixed-rate debt can delay household effects, while floating-rate borrowers react sooner. Supply shocks can create difficult trade-offs between inflation and activity.|Interest-rate channel: Changes financing and saving incentives.;;Credit/asset channel: Changes lending and balance-sheet conditions.;;Expectations/FX channel: Changes anticipated prices and currency conditions.|A floating-rate borrower owes ₹100 lakh and its rate rises from 8% to 10%.~~Annual interest increases ₹2.;;Disposable cash for spending or investment falls accordingly if income is unchanged.;;Compare fixed-rate borrowers whose payments reset later.~~Transmission depends on contract structure and timing.~~Currency and demand responses require additional analysis.|Map policy channels.;;Inspect repricing schedules.;;Consider lags and supply constraints.
T32.06|Policy rates guide short-term financial conditions; reserve requirements specify eligible balances under local regimes; open-market operations transact in assets to implement policy and manage liquidity. They are different instruments with different direct balance-sheet effects.~~Commercial loan rates also include credit, term and operating costs. A policy-rate change need not pass through one-for-one to every borrower.|Policy rate: Central-bank target or administered rate.;;Reserve requirement: Required eligible balance rule.;;Open-market operation: Central-bank asset transaction.|A borrower pays policy-linked base 6% plus credit spread 3%.~~Initial rate is 9%.;;If base rises to 7% but spread falls to 2.5%, total is 9.5%.;;Analyse both components.~~Borrowing rates reflect more than policy alone.~~Reserve and operating frameworks differ across countries.|Identify the local framework.;;Separate base and spread.;;Trace actual bank and borrower effects.
T32.07|Quantitative easing purchases assets to influence financial conditions, generally creating central-bank reserves in the process. Quantitative tightening reduces holdings through runoff or sales under the chosen programme. Neither is identical to government spending or a direct transfer to every household.~~Market impact depends on expectations, asset supply, term premiums and broader conditions. A central-bank balance-sheet change does not mechanically imply a fixed stock-market response.|Asset purchase: Acquires securities under a programme.;;Runoff: Allows holdings to mature without full reinvestment.;;Active sale: Sells holdings to reduce the portfolio.|A central bank buys ₹100 of bonds from a nonbank through its bank.~~Central-bank assets rise ₹100.;;Bank reserves rise and the seller receives a deposit under the simplified settlement.;;Track subsequent portfolio choices separately.~~The first effect is a balance-sheet exchange.~~Its economic consequences depend on behaviour and market conditions.|Trace who sells and receives cash.;;Separate accounting from transmission.;;Assess term premiums and expectations.
T32.08|Fiscal policy uses government spending, taxes and transfers to influence resources and demand. Automatic stabilisers respond without new legislation, while discretionary measures are deliberate changes. Effects depend on economic slack, financing, imports and implementation.~~A headline spending amount is not necessarily the net stimulus: tax changes, saving and displaced activity can offset it. Public projects should also be judged on long-run value.|Spending: Purchases goods, services or investment.;;Tax/transfer policy: Changes disposable resources.;;Automatic stabiliser: Responds to the economic cycle.|A fictional programme raises spending ₹20 lakh and taxes ₹8 in the same period.~~Direct net budget expansion is ₹12.;;Assess who receives and pays the amounts.;;Model demand effects without assuming a fixed multiplier.~~Budget arithmetic and economic impact are different.~~Timing, capacity and financing affect the result.|Identify gross and net changes.;;Assess recipients and timing.;;Examine long-run costs and benefits.
T32.09|A fiscal deficit is a period flow when defined expenditure exceeds revenue; public debt is an accumulated stock of obligations. Debt changes can also reflect valuation and other adjustments. Primary balance excludes interest under the relevant convention, helping distinguish current policy from past borrowing costs.~~Compare consistent government coverage and GDP denominators. A deficit ratio alone does not describe maturity, currency or refinancing risk.|Overall deficit: Includes interest in the balance.;;Primary balance: Excludes interest.;;Debt stock: Outstanding obligations at a date.|Government revenue is ₹100, noninterest spending ₹110 and interest ₹10.~~Primary deficit is ₹10.;;Overall deficit is ₹20.;;Reconcile borrowing and other debt-stock adjustments.~~Interest widens the overall financing need.~~National definitions and consolidation can differ.|Use consistent coverage.;;Separate primary and overall balances.;;Inspect debt composition and maturity.
T32.10|Debt sustainability examines whether a borrower can service obligations without implausible adjustments or disruptive restructuring. For governments, interest-growth dynamics, primary balances, currency, maturity and institutional credibility matter. Domestic-currency issuance reduces some risks but does not eliminate inflation or financing constraints.~~A debt ratio can rise even with stable nominal debt if GDP falls. Use scenarios rather than one universal safe threshold.|Interest-growth effect: Changes debt burden relative to output.;;Primary balance effect: Adds to or reduces borrowing need.;;Funding risk: Ability to refinance and service maturities.|Debt is ₹100 and GDP ₹200, so ratio is 50%; next period debt rises 5% and GDP 2%.~~New ratio is 105 ÷ 204 ≈ 51.47%.;;Add primary-balance and other adjustments in a fuller model.;;Stress rates and currency.~~Debt growing faster than GDP raises the ratio.~~No universal sustainability conclusion follows from this alone.|Model debt dynamics.;;Inspect currency and maturity.;;Test realistic fiscal and growth scenarios.
T32.11|Balance-of-payments accounts record transactions between residents and nonresidents. The current account includes trade and income flows; capital and financial accounts record other transfers and financial transactions under official conventions. Double-entry accounting links flows, but signs must be read carefully.~~A current-account deficit does not automatically imply immediate crisis; financing quality, external balance sheets and currency structure matter.|Current account: Goods, services and specified income/transfers.;;Financial account: Changes in cross-border financial positions.;;Reserves/errors: Official reserve transactions and statistical reconciliation.|A simplified economy imports ₹20 more than it exports and has no other current flows.~~Current balance is −₹20.;;Identify matching financing or reserve movements.;;Distinguish stable investment from short-term borrowing.~~The same deficit can have different funding risks.~~Actual accounts contain income, transfers and convention-specific signs.|Use official sign conventions.;;Examine financing composition.;;Connect flows with external asset and liability stocks.
T32.12|Exchange-rate regimes determine how a currency's price is set or managed. Floating regimes allow market movement; fixed regimes seek a stated parity; managed arrangements intervene to varying degrees. Maintaining a peg can require reserves, policy adjustments or controls.~~A fixed quotation does not remove devaluation or convertibility risk. Monetary autonomy, exchange-rate stability and open capital flows create policy trade-offs.|Floating: Market-driven exchange price.;;Fixed/pegged: Commitment to a reference rate.;;Managed: Intervention within a chosen framework.|A central bank defends an illustrative peg while private demand for foreign currency exceeds supply by ₹10 equivalent.~~It may supply reserves under the simplified mechanism.;;Repeated intervention reduces available reserves.;;Assess policy and confidence responses.~~A stable observed price can coexist with underlying pressure.~~Actual intervention tools and convertibility rules vary.|Identify the actual regime.;;Track reserves and restrictions.;;Stress devaluation and funding exposure.
T32.13|Sovereign credit analysis assesses willingness and ability to service government obligations. Economic capacity, institutions, fiscal position, external funding and debt structure all matter. A rating is an opinion under a methodology, not a repayment guarantee.~~Domestic and foreign-currency obligations can have different risk characteristics. Market spreads also reflect liquidity and investor sentiment.|Fiscal capacity: Revenue and budget flexibility.;;External capacity: Foreign-currency resources and funding.;;Institutional willingness: Policy credibility and repayment incentives.|A sovereign owes ₹100 equivalent foreign-currency debt and its currency depreciates 20% in domestic terms.~~Domestic-currency burden rises to ₹120 if debt quantity is unchanged.;;Assess foreign-currency export and reserve resources.;;Review maturity and refinancing needs.~~Currency mismatch can amplify debt pressure.~~Nominal GDP and other responses affect the full ratio.|Review debt by currency.;;Assess institutions and revenue capacity.;;Use ratings alongside independent analysis.
T32.14|Financial repression describes policies that channel savings or constrain financial prices and choices in ways that can support public financing. Capital controls restrict specified cross-border transactions. Their legal forms and economic effects vary, and they can change access, yields and currency risk.~~Nominal returns may appear stable while inflation erodes value or transfers are restricted. Analyse actual rules instead of relying on the label.|Rate restraint: Limits or influences financial pricing.;;Directed holdings: Requires or incentivises certain assets.;;Capital control: Restricts specified international flows.|A deposit earns 3% while inflation is 8%.~~Real return is 1.03 ÷ 1.08 − 1 ≈ −4.63%.;;If transfer restrictions apply, assess liquidity separately.;;Identify the relevant policy rather than assuming its cause.~~Stable nominal balances can lose real value.~~The example is hypothetical and not a claim about a current country.|Read current primary rules.;;Measure real returns.;;Assess convertibility and allocation constraints.
T32.15|Leading indicators seek early evidence of future activity, while coincident and lagging measures describe current or past conditions. Forecasts combine data with assumptions and models; publication lags and revisions can change conclusions. No indicator predicts every turning point reliably.~~Use information available at the forecast date. Revised data can make a historical forecasting rule appear more successful than it was in real time.|Leading: Tends to move before activity.;;Coincident: Moves with activity.;;Lagging: Responds after activity changes.|A forecast uses a monthly index published six weeks later to claim it predicted the previous month's decline.~~Record when the data actually became available.;;Rebuild the forecast with contemporaneous inputs.;;Compare with a simple benchmark.~~Publication timing can invalidate apparent foresight.~~Indicator relationships can change across regimes.|Track release dates and revisions.;;Use multiple indicators.;;Evaluate forecasts against realistic benchmarks.
T32.16|Macro conditions affect companies through demand, costs, financing, currency and valuation. The effect differs by business model: higher rates can hurt leveraged borrowers while changing bank margins in more complex ways. Portfolio exposures should map to economic drivers rather than a single macro slogan.~~Translate scenarios into cash-flow and discount-rate assumptions consistently. Do not count the same shock twice without explaining both channels.|Operating channel: Sales, wages and input costs.;;Financing channel: Interest, credit and liquidity.;;Valuation channel: Required returns and expected growth.|A company has ₹50 lakh floating debt; rates rise 2 points while annual contribution falls ₹3.~~Interest increases ₹1.;;Pretax profit falls ₹4 before other effects.;;Assess investment, working capital and valuation separately.~~Multiple macro channels combine in company outcomes.~~The response depends on pricing power and hedges.|Map drivers by company.;;Use coherent scenarios.;;Separate cash-flow and discount-rate effects.
